Component layers.0.mlp.down_proj:2767 fires on the finite verb that comes right after a
noun phrase's head noun: "the speaker [says]", "Students [reported]", "the owner [must]",
"the company [has]", "the commissioner [said]". Those are the subject-verb boundaries of
ordinary English clauses. It is silent on the same words after a pronoun or a function word
("it is", "this was", "there are", "which can") and on plural nouns, "to", and other
non-verbs.

The program predicts, per token:
  * modals (will, can, must, ...) and auxiliaries (has, had, were, is, ...) after a noun:
    strong, with had/has/have/were strongest and is/are/was weaker;
  * a short list of common finite verbs (says, said, shows, thinks, reported, ...) after a noun;
  * the same after he/she/they at 30% strength (weak in the data);
  * zero otherwise.
Everything depends on the current token and the one before it; nothing is carried.
"""

READS = ()

MODALS = {"will", "can", "must", "may", "should", "would", "shall", "could", "might"}
AUXILIARY_VALUE = {
    "had": 0.9, "were": 0.8, "has": 0.75, "have": 0.75,
    "is": 0.45, "are": 0.45, "was": 0.45, "does": 0.35, "do": 0.2, "did": 0.2,
}
FINITE_VERBS = {
    "says", "say", "said", "shows", "show", "showed", "thinks", "think", "thought", "performs",
    "starts", "gets", "takes", "wants", "needs", "need", "feels", "notes", "noted", "looks",
    "requires", "reported", "told", "tells", "claimed", "claims", "stated", "mentions", "speaks",
    "wrote", "became", "becomes", "began", "came", "went", "saw", "felt", "received", "offers",
    "allows", "works", "uses", "makes", "gives", "owns", "decides", "moves", "prefers",
    "explains", "knows", "believes", "agrees", "refuses", "suffers", "reaches", "rolls",
    "grows", "remains",
}
# Words that are not noun subjects: pronouns, determiners, prepositions, conjunctions,
# auxiliaries and modals themselves.
NOT_NOUN = {
    "it", "you", "i", "we", "there", "this", "that", "which", "who", "what", "how", "these",
    "those", "me", "him", "her", "them", "us", "the", "a", "an", "of", "in", "on", "at", "to",
    "for", "with", "by", "from", "and", "or", "but", "not", "as", "so", "than", "then", "also",
    "if", "when", "while", "because", "just", "only", "very", "more", "most", "all", "some",
    "no", "here", "will", "can", "must", "may", "should", "would", "shall", "could", "might",
    "has", "have", "had", "is", "are", "was", "were", "does", "do", "did",
}
PRONOUN_SUBJECTS = {"he", "she", "they"}
PRONOUN_WEIGHT = 0.3
VERB_VALUE = 0.6
MODAL_VALUE = 0.7


def word(text):
    """The token as a lowercase word, or None if it is not a plain alphabetic word."""
    text = text.strip()
    return text.lower() if text.isalpha() else None


def step(carry, prefix, upstream):
    # Only look back one token, and never across a document boundary.
    if len(prefix.token_strings) < 2 or prefix.document_ids[-2] != prefix.document_ids[-1]:
        return None, 0.0
    cur = word(prefix.token_strings[-1])
    prev = word(prefix.token_strings[-2])
    if cur is None or prev is None:
        return None, 0.0
    if cur in MODALS:
        value = MODAL_VALUE
    elif cur in AUXILIARY_VALUE:
        value = AUXILIARY_VALUE[cur]
    elif cur in FINITE_VERBS:
        value = VERB_VALUE
    else:
        return None, 0.0
    if prev in PRONOUN_SUBJECTS:
        value *= PRONOUN_WEIGHT
    elif prev in NOT_NOUN:
        return None, 0.0
    return None, value

